 # Monte Carlo Integration
-Also called: Monte Carlo estimation, sample averaging estimator
+
+Also called: Monte Carlo Estimation, Sample Mean Estimator
 
 ## Overview
-Monte Carlo Integration is a numerical technique for estimating definite integrals by averaging function evaluations at random points weighted by their probability density function (pdf) [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of computing high-dimensional integrals that lack closed-form analytical solutions, such as those arising in light transport simulation [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from deterministic quadrature methods by maintaining a convergence rate of $O(1/\sqrt{N})$ regardless of the integral's dimension or the smoothness of the integrand, making it the standard approach for rendering equations [S1, unofficial].
+Monte Carlo Integration is a technique to estimate integrals by using probabilities, evaluating an integral multiple times at random points weighted by their probability density function (pdf) and averaging the results [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of computing high-dimensional integrals, such as those arising in the Path Integral Framework for light transport, where analytical solutions are unavailable [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from deterministic quadrature methods by its convergence rate of $O(1/\sqrt{N})$ in any dimension, regardless of the smoothness of the function, making it the standard approach for rendering equations [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
