 ### [Monte Carlo Estimator of Edge Integrals](10285-monte-carlo-estimator-of-edge-integrals.md)
-Estimates edge integrals from radiance discontinuities at geometric boundaries. Chosen when standard path tracing misses contributions from sharp edges or surface boundaries.
+This method estimates the edge integrals that arise from discontinuities in radiance across boundary curves in participating media[10285-monte-carlo-estimator-of-edge-integrals.md]. It distinguishes itself from standard volumetric path tracing by explicitly handling the discontinuities in surface emission and scattering that occur when a ray intersects a boundary curve or sharp edge, ensuring unbiased estimation of the radiance jump[10285-monte-carlo-estimator-of-edge-integrals.md].
 
 ### [Residual Color Estimation Scheme](10577-residual-color-estimation-scheme.md)
-Reduces bias in Fourier coefficient calculations due to nonuniform sampling. Chosen when spectral integration requires correction for DC component bias.
+The Residual Color Estimation Scheme is a Monte Carlo integration technique designed to reduce estimation bias in Fourier coefficient calculation caused by nonuniform sampling[10577-residual-color-estimation-scheme.md]. It sets itself apart from the standard Monte Carlo Integration by explicitly subtracting the estimated direct current (DC) component from the signal before integration, and from the Monte Carlo Estimator of Edge Integrals by focusing on spectral coefficient bias rather than edge integral estimation[10577-residual-color-estimation-scheme.md].
+
+### Monte Carlo Estimation of Derivatives
+This variant provides Monte Carlo estimates for path integrals involving BSSRDF and BRDF lobes. For a path integral with BSSRDF, the estimate is $\langle I_3 \rangle = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \frac{\hat{f}(p_0 p_1 p_2 p_3)}{p(y_1)p(y_2)}$ [S2]. For a path integral with BRDF, the estimate is $\langle I_2 \rangle = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \frac{\hat{f}(p_0 p_1 p_2)}{p(y_1)}$ [S2].
+
+### Truncated Ellipsoid Primitive Projected Area Estimation
+An efficient Monte Carlo estimator to calculate the projected area of the intersection of a cube and an ellipsoid [S3, unofficial]. It uniformly samples points on the visible ellipsoid by sampling on a unit disk, lifting to the upper hemisphere, warping via Cholesky decomposition of the inverse ellipsoid matrix block, and transforming to world space [S3, unofficial]. A fixed sample budget of $N=16$ is used in practice [S3, unofficial]. An early-exit optimization skips the intersection test if the ellipsoid is fully contained by the cube or vice versa [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of samples. Each sample requires one evaluation of $f(x)$ and one evaluation of $p(x)$.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space, excluding storage for the samples if processed in a streaming fashion.
-- **Convergence Rate:** $O(1/\sqrt{N})$ in any dimension [S1, unofficial]. This rate is independent of the dimensionality of the integral and the smoothness of the function, which is a significant advantage over deterministic methods that suffer from the curse of dimensionality.
-- **Strengths:** Dimensionality independence, simplicity of implementation, and unbiased nature (assuming correct sampling).
-- **Limitations:** Slow convergence rate compared to deterministic methods in low dimensions for smooth functions; high variance if importance sampling is not used effectively.
-- **When to use:** High-dimensional integrals, complex domains, or when the integrand is not smooth or has discontinuities.
-- **When not to use:** Low-dimensional integrals with smooth functions where deterministic quadrature offers faster convergence.
